@@ -0,0 +1,385 @@
+# YAQA + REAL-Q w_clip quantizer
+
+## 1. Scope
+
+`YAQA_wclip` is a copy of the working `YAQA` tree at upstream commit
+`f9508723251ad839f0162326569f17fe70486fcc`, including the existing
+256×2048 WikiText-2 calibration, A/K/V-aware Hessian, hfize, and evaluation
+adaptations.
+
+This fork changes the weight quantizer only:
+
+- QTIP/Bitshift trellis quantization is replaced by a uniform INT2/INT3/INT4
+  leaf quantizer matching REAL-Q with `w_clip=1`.
+- YAQA still performs its native two-sided randomized Hadamard processing,
+  uses both `Hin` and `Hout`, traverses blocks in the same anti-diagonal order,
+  and applies the same three rectangular feedback terms.
+- REAL-Q's GPTQ column loop, `act_order`, one-sided Hessian update, and
+  compensation code are deliberately not imported.
+
+No full model experiment was run while implementing this fork. Unit, CUDA
+bit-exact, CLI, and serialization smoke tests were run on
+`j-8j1en3m0aq`.
+
+## 2. Exact quantizer definition
+
+The implementation is
+[`lib/codebook/wclip.py`](lib/codebook/wclip.py).
+
+The configuration is frozen to:
+
+| Field | Value |
+|---|---:|
+| `bits` | 2, 3, or 4 |
+| `perchannel` | true, meaning independent output rows |
+| `sym` | true |
+| `mse` / `w_clip` | true |
+| `norm` | 2.4 |
+| `grid` | 50 |
+| `maxshrink` | 0.5 |
+| `groupsize` | 128 |
+| search | `cartesian_legacy` |
+| update implementation | `guarded` |
+| tie rule | strict `<`, first winner retained |
+| zero point | 0 |
+
+Despite the historical “MSE” name, the REAL-Q default objective is
+
+```text
+sum(abs(QDQ(weight) - weight) ** 2.4)
+```
+
+It is not strict squared error.
+
+The signed code ranges are:
+
+| Weight bits | `maxq` used as scale denominator | Stored integer range |
+|---:|---:|---:|
+| 4 | 7 | `[-8, 7]` |
+| 3 | 3 | `[-4, 3]` |
+| 2 | 1 | `[-2, 1]` |
+
+For every row/group, the legacy search evaluates `25 × 25 = 625` endpoint
+pairs. `i,j` both range from 0 through 24, so the smallest endpoint multiplier
+is `1 - 24/50 = 0.52`. Ties do not replace the first winner.
+
+## 3. G128 semantics
+
+G128 has the same meaning as REAL-Q:
+
+```text
+weight shape: [out_features, in_features]
+group id:     natural_input_column // 128
+scale shape:  [out_features, ceil(in_features / 128)]
+```
+
+- Each output row has independent scales.
+- Every contiguous 128 natural input columns in that row share one scale.
+- Rows are never flattened together.
+- The standalone leaf quantizer supports a final short group at its real
+  width without padding.
+- The scale is fitted once on the complete FP32 `Wr`, before YAQA feedback,
+  then remains frozen throughout rounding.
+
+The controlled end-to-end LDLQ entry uses `td_y=128`, so it requires every
+projection input dimension to be divisible by 128. Llama-3.2 1B/3B satisfy
+this condition; preflight rejects other architectures. Short-tail behavior is
+tested for the reusable leaf quantizer but is not claimed for this controlled
+end-to-end path.
+
+Here, `Wr` is the weight after YAQA's own left/right sign and Hadamard
+transform. “Natural column” therefore means the unpermuted column coordinate
+of this YAQA quantizer-domain matrix. There is no REAL-Q `act_order`
+permutation in this fork.
+
+YAQA defines `td_x/td_y` as quantizer block sizes. Its paper uses
+`g_x=1, g_y=32` for a per-row G32 ordinary INT4 quantizer. The corresponding
+G128 configuration is therefore:
+
+```text
+td_x = 1
+td_y = 128
+```
+
+This changes the quantizer block configuration, not the YAQA algorithm. The
+old `16×16` values were the QTIP vector-codebook block and are not used for
+ordinary per-row G128 INT.
+
+## 4. Where the integration happens
+
+The sequence for each projection is:
+
+1. Load and normalize the original YAQA `Hin` and `Hout`.
+2. Apply YAQA's native two-sided RHT to the Hessians and weight.
+3. Fit REAL-Q-compatible G128 scales once on FP32 `Wr`.
+4. Run the unchanged YAQA anti-diagonal/two-sided feedback loop.
+5. At the leaf projection, select `scale[row, column // 128]`, then apply:
+
+   ```text
+   code = clamp(round(target / scale), qmin, qmax)
+   dequantized = scale * code
+   ```
+
+6. Apply YAQA's inverse RHT and save the resulting dense fake-dequantized
+   weight.
+
+Only the leaf call in
+[`lib/algo/ldlq.py`](lib/algo/ldlq.py) receives tile coordinates so it can
+select the correct frozen row/group scale. The target construction, three
+feedback terms, anti-diagonal schedule, and writeback order are unchanged.
+
+The former QTIP global `Wscale`, Viterbi trellis, `pack_trellis`, QTIP kernel,
+and QTIP `QuantizedLinear` are bypassed because they do not represent ordinary
+uniform integers.
+
+## 5. Output format and runtime boundary
+
+Every raw projection file contains:
+
+- `qweight`: signed `int8 [out_features, in_features]` codes. Values occupy
+  only the requested 2/3/4-bit range, but they are not bit-packed.
+- `scale`: FP32 compact scales with shape
+  `[out_features, ceil(in_features / 128)]`.
+- `SU`, `SV`: YAQA RHT signs.
+- `weight`: the dense BF16/FP16 fake-dequantized weight after inverse RHT.
+- quantizer metadata and one-/two-sided proxy errors.
+
+The hfized model uses ordinary dense `nn.Linear` weights copied from
+`weight`. This follows REAL-Q's quality-evaluation convention of persisting
+fake-dequantized model weights. The custom YAQA Llama loader is retained so a
+W2A4KV4 model still installs all A/V hooks and the post-RoPE K quantizer.
+
+This first implementation is for algorithm/quality comparison. It does not
+claim:
+
+- packed 2/3/4-bit storage;
+- a uniform-INT CUDA inference kernel;
+- low-bit runtime memory or throughput.
+
+If scales were stored as FP16 in a packed implementation, the theoretical
+rates at G128 would be W4=4.125, W3=3.125, and W2=2.125 bits/weight. The raw
+artifacts here intentionally retain FP32 scales and dense weights for exact
+auditing, so their on-disk size must not be reported as those theoretical
+rates.
+
+## 6. Running tests
+
+On the shared experimental machine:
+
+```bash
+ssh -tt \
+  'u-7s51rmjf43+j-8j1en3m0aq-master-0.canoe-jobs.pod@pa-cne02-prod-01-ssh.xaminim.com'
+
+cd /minimax-avatar-new/zhangqian/realq/gptq_plus
+YAQA_wclip/scripts/run_tests.sh
+```
+
+The test script runs:
+
+- INT2/3/4 range checks;
+- G128 row/natural-column and short-tail checks;
+- exact CPU scale/code/QDQ comparison with REAL-Q `WeightQuantizer`;
+- tile coordinate and frozen-scale LDLQ checks;
+- dense runtime projection and hfize loader checks;
+- Hessian-aware/A16 provenance and fresh-output checks;
+- codes/scales/SU/SV dense reconstruction and HF-tree freshness checks;
+- an additional CUDA bit-exact comparison with REAL-Q.
+
+Implementation-time result on 2026-07-25:
+
+```text
+17 passed in 14.57s
+CUDA exact: W2, scales=(3, 3), codes=[-2, 1]
+CUDA exact: W3, scales=(3, 3), codes=[-4, 3]
+CUDA exact: W4, scales=(3, 3), codes=[-8, 7]
+```
+
+## 7. Quantization commands
+
+Existing validated Hessians can be reused because Hessian collection is
+unchanged. W4/W3 use A16/KV16 Hessians; W2A4KV4 must use the aware Hessians
+collected with A/K/V fake quantization before Hessian precomputation.
+
+Example: 1B W4A16KV16
+
+```bash
+cd /minimax-avatar-new/zhangqian/realq/gptq_plus
+
+YAQA_wclip/scripts/run_quantize.sh \
+  1b W4A16KV16 0 \
+  output/yaqa_compare_20260725/j-8j1en3m0aq/hessians/llama32_1b_a16kv16_n256_ieeefp32v1 \
+  output/yaqa_compare_20260725/j-8j1en3m0aq/validation/llama32_1b_a16kv16_n256_ieeefp32v1.json \
+  output/yaqa_wclip/raw/llama32_1b_w4_g128
+```
+
+Example: 3B W3A16KV16 on GPUs 0–3
+
+```bash
+YAQA_wclip/scripts/run_quantize.sh \
+  3b W3A16KV16 0,1,2,3 \
+  output/yaqa_compare_20260725/j-8j1en3m0aq/hessians/llama32_3b_a16kv16_n256_ieeefp32v1 \
+  output/yaqa_compare_20260725/j-8j1en3m0aq/validation/llama32_3b_a16kv16_n256_ieeefp32v1.json \
+  output/yaqa_wclip/raw/llama32_3b_w3_g128
+```
+
+Example: 1B W2A4KV4-aware
+
+```bash
+YAQA_wclip/scripts/run_quantize.sh \
+  1b W2A4KV4 0 \
+  output/yaqa_compare_20260725/j-8j1en3m0aq/hessians/llama32_1b_a4kv4_aware_n256_ieeefp32v1 \
+  output/yaqa_compare_20260725/j-8j1en3m0aq/validation/llama32_1b_a4kv4_aware_n256_ieeefp32v1.json \
+  output/yaqa_wclip/raw/llama32_1b_w2a4kv4_aware_g128
+```
+
+The wrapper fixes:
+
+- WikiText-2 calibration to 256 sequences × 2048 tokens;
+- the existing frozen token artifact and SHA256;
+- `w_groupsize=128`;
+- `td_x=1`, `td_y=128`;
+- FP32 REAL-Q-compatible scale search;
+- `ft_epochs=0`;
+- W2 A/K/V-aware metadata and clip 0.9.
+
+Before loading the model, the entry verifies the schema-v2 Hessian validation
+report, model identity, complete file set and SHA256 values, A/K/V topology,
+256×2048 calibration token identity, and IEEE-FP32 collection metadata. The
+raw output must be a new path. A partial failed run is intentionally never
+resumed silently; use a different fresh output path after diagnosing it.
+
+## 8. hfize, validate, and evaluate
+
+hfize to a fresh output directory:
+
+```bash
+YAQA_wclip/scripts/run_hfize.sh \
+  output/yaqa_wclip/raw/llama32_1b_w4_g128 \
+  output/yaqa_wclip/hf/llama32_1b_w4_g128
+```
+
+Validate raw config/layernorms, integer ranges, G128 scales, exact
+codes/scales/SU/SV-to-dense reconstruction, dense hfize parity, tokenizer
+parity, A/K/V deployment topology, two GPU forwards, and deterministic reload:
+
+```bash
+YAQA_wclip/scripts/run_validate_hfized.sh \
+  1b W4A16KV16 \
+  output/yaqa_wclip/raw/llama32_1b_w4_g128 \
+  output/yaqa_wclip/hf/llama32_1b_w4_g128 \
+  output/yaqa_wclip/validation/llama32_1b_w4_g128.json \
+  0
+```
+
+Run the unchanged REAL-Q canonical evaluator:
+
+```bash
+YAQA_wclip/scripts/run_evaluate.sh \
+  1b W4A16KV16 \
+  output/yaqa_wclip/hf/llama32_1b_w4_g128 \
+  output/yaqa_wclip/validation/llama32_1b_w4_g128.json \
+  output/yaqa_wclip/results/llama32_1b_w4_g128.json \
+  0
+```
+
+This evaluator keeps the agreed protocol:
+
+- WikiText-2 test, non-overlapping 2048-token chunks;
+- FP32 PPL;
+- full-vocabulary FP32 `KL(FP || quantized)`;
+- REAL-Q's ten lm-eval 0.4.4 tasks;
+- zero-shot, no chat template, batch size 32.
+
+The wrapper first re-hashes the complete HF file tree against the validation
+report, so modifying a model after validation fails before the unchanged
+canonical evaluator starts.
+
+## 9. Problems encountered and resolutions
+
+### Direct SSH by job ID did not resolve
+
+`ssh j-8j1en3m0aq` fails because a Canoe job ID is not a DNS hostname. The
+working target is:
+
+```text
+{creator_id}+{job_id}-master-0.canoe-jobs.pod@{cluster_name}-ssh.xaminim.com
+```
+
+The Canoe job and task were confirmed Running before connecting.
+
+### Canoe SSH needs a PTY
+
+Passing a remote command without a PTY returned only `No PTY requested`.
+Opening with `ssh -tt` and then running commands in the interactive shell
+works.
+
+### Host Python could not run PyTorch tests
+
+The development host's default Python had no PyTorch, and the shared `.venv`
+points to Python 3.12 packages that are only usable inside the experimental
+image. Tests were therefore run in `j-8j1en3m0aq`, as requested, without
+installing or changing host packages.
+
+### Remote pytest created a root-owned bytecode cache
+
+The experimental shell runs as root, so pytest created
+`YAQA_wclip/tests/__pycache__` as root. A later host-side `py_compile` tried
+to replace one of those generated files and got `Permission denied`; this was
+not a source-code or test failure. Static compilation is run with a temporary
+`PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX`, which keeps bytecode outside the shared source tree and
+avoids changing ownership of either source files or remote test caches.
+
+### The job's system Python had an incompatible Transformers version
+
+The image's default `python` lacked
+`transformers.models.qwen3`, which REAL-Q's current utility module imports.
+Using the workspace interpreter, `.venv/bin/python`, provides the frozen
+compatible environment (`torch 2.9.1`, `transformers 4.56.2`).
+
+### Root `datasets/` can shadow Hugging Face datasets
+
+The workspace contains a data directory named `datasets/`. Importing broad
+YAQA utility modules unnecessarily during the leaf LDLQ unit test caused it
+to shadow the Python package in one environment. Unused `copy`, `os`, `glog`,
+and `lib.utils` imports were removed from `ldlq.py`; production data loading
+still uses the proper package under the workspace `.venv`.
+
+### QTIP packing cannot store ordinary INT codes
+
+`pack_trellis` encodes QTIP Viterbi state transitions, and its runtime always
+constructs `BitshiftLinear`. Feeding scalar integer codes into that path would
+silently represent a different weight. The w_clip path therefore bypasses
+trellis packing and publishes a dense fake-dequantized quality model while
+retaining auditable integer codes and scales.
+
+### G128 is not the former QTIP 16×16 block
+
+The initial temptation was to keep `td_x=td_y=16` and only share scales across
+eight tiles. The YAQA paper explicitly defines these values as quantizer block
+sizes and uses `g_x=1, g_y=groupsize` for ordinary groupwise INT. The fork
+therefore uses `1×128`, preserving the YAQA algorithm while selecting the
+correct block configuration for the replacement quantizer.
+
+### A Hessian directory name is not sufficient provenance
+
+The first wrapper draft accepted any existing Hessian directory. That could
+silently pair W2A4KV4-aware quantization with A16 Hessians. Quantization now
+requires the formal schema-v2 validation report and rechecks its model,
+A/K/V topology, token identity, IEEE-FP32 metadata, file set, sizes, and
+SHA256 values before consuming the Hessians.
+
+### Reusing an output directory can mix configurations
+
+Upstream `check_exist` supports resuming complete layers. Without a bound
+fingerprint, rerunning with a different setting could preserve old projection
+files while overwriting `config.pt`. The controlled wrapper and Python entry
+now require a fresh raw output path, and hfize/validation/evaluation outputs
+are also fresh or hash-checked as appropriate.
+
+### REAL-Q `act_order` qualification
+
+The frozen full-matrix G128 observer matches the formal REAL-Q
+`act_order=true` route used for this comparison. REAL-Q's
+`act_order=false` GPTQ route can refit scales on sequentially updated blocks.
+That GPTQ-specific behavior is deliberately not copied because this fork
+replaces only the leaf quantizer and keeps YAQA's own two-sided feedback.
